{"word": "win", "source": "freedictionaryapi", "data": {"word": "win", "entries": [{"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "verb", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/wɪn/", "tags": []}], "forms": [{"word": "wins", "tags": ["present", "singular", "third-person"]}, {"word": "winning", "tags": ["participle", "present"]}, {"word": "won", "tags": ["past"]}, {"word": "wan", "tags": ["obsolete", "past"]}, {"word": "won", "tags": ["participle", "past"]}, {"word": "no-table-tags", "tags": ["table-tags"]}, {"word": "glossary", "tags": ["inflection-template"]}, {"word": "win", "tags": ["infinitive"]}, {"word": "win", "tags": ["first-person", "present", "singular"]}, {"word": "won", "tags": ["first-person", "past", "singular"]}, {"word": "win", "tags": ["present", "second-person", "singular"]}, {"word": "winnest", "tags": ["archaic", "present", "second-person", "singular"]}, {"word": "won", "tags": ["past", "second-person", "singular"]}, {"word": "wonnest", "tags": ["archaic", "past", "second-person", "singular"]}, {"word": "wins", "tags": ["present", "singular", "third-person"]}, {"word": "winneth", "tags": ["archaic", "present", "singular", "third-person"]}, {"word": "won", "tags": ["past", "singular", "third-person"]}, {"word": "win", "tags": ["plural", "present"]}, {"word": "won", "tags": ["past", "plural"]}, {"word": "win", "tags": ["present", "subjunctive"]}, {"word": "won", "tags": ["past", "subjunctive"]}, {"word": "win", "tags": ["imperative", "present"]}, {"word": "-", "tags": ["imperative", "past"]}, {"word": "winning", "tags": ["participle", "present"]}, {"word": "won", "tags": ["participle", "past"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "(obsolete, transitive) To conquer, defeat.", "tags": ["obsolete", "transitive"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "For and we doo bataille we two wyl fyghte with one knyȝt at ones\n and therfore yf ye wille fyghte ſoo we wille be redy at what houre ye wille aſſigne\n And yf ye wynne vs in bataille the lady ſhal haue her landes ageyne\n ye ſay wel ſayd ſir Vwayne\n therfor make yow redy ſo that ye be here to morne in the defence of the ladyes ryght", "reference": "1485, Sir Thomas Malory, Le Morte Darthur, Book IV:"}, {"text": "For the glory, the power to win the Black Lord, I will search for the Emerald Sword.", "reference": "1998, Rhapsody, Emerald Sword:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(transitive, intransitive) To reach some destination or object, despite difficulty or toil (now usually intransitive, with preposition or locative adverb).", "tags": ["intransitive", "transitive"], "examples": ["I well may gang out, love, but I'll never win home."], "quotes": [{"text": "No sooner did I get my freedom than my footsteps began to be dogged by the spies of the Iron Heel. It was necessary that they should be thrown off the track, and that I should win to California.", "reference": "1908, Jack London, The Iron Heel:"}, {"text": "As this position was vulnerable, a trench was immediately begun from the junction of the Green Line with Lager Alley, back to the old British front line, in order to form a defensive flank for the protection of the troops of the 5th Infantry Brigade who had won through to their objective.", "reference": "1922, Everard Wyrall, The History of the Second Division 1914-1918:"}, {"text": "Parson Brooke was transferred in a couple of years to the Southwark mint, on dissolution of which he won back to the Tower, there to experiment with machinery in Mary's reign.", "reference": "1953, John Craig, The Mint: A History of the London Mint from A.D. 287 to 1948:"}, {"text": "That euen in the Porch he him did win,\n And cleft his head aſunder to his chin", "reference": "1596, Edmund Spenser, “Book VI, Canto I”, in The Faerie Queene. […], London: […] [John Wolfe] for William Ponsonbie, →OCLC, stanza 23:"}, {"text": "And when the stony path began,\n By which the naked peak they won,\n Up flew the snowy ptarmigan.", "reference": "1808 February 22, Walter Scott, “(please specify the introduction or canto number, or chapter name)”, in Marmion; a Tale of Flodden Field, Edinburgh: […] J[ames] Ballantyne and Co. for Archibald Constable and Company, […]; London: William Miller, and John Murray, →OCLC:"}, {"text": "“Has he nae friends?” said she, in a tearful voice.\n“That has he so!” cried Alan, “if we could but win to them!—friends and rich friends, beds to lie in, food to eat, doctors to see to him—and here he must tramp in the dubs and sleep in the heather like a beggarman.”", "reference": "1886 May 1 – July 31, Robert Louis Stevenson, Kidnapped, being Memoirs of the Adventures of David Balfour in the Year 1751: […], London; Paris: Cassell & Company, published 1886, →OCLC:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(transitive) To triumph or achieve victory in (a game, a war, etc.).", "tags": ["transitive"], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(transitive) To gain (a prize) by succeeding in competition or contest.", "tags": ["transitive"], "examples": ["to win the jackpot in a lottery;  to win a bottle of wine in a raffle"], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [{"language": {"code": "sq", "name": "Albanian"}, "word": "përfitoj"}, {"language": {"code": "ar", "name": "Arabic"}, "word": "رَبِحَ"}, {"language": {"code": "acw", "name": "Arabic"}, "word": "كسب"}, {"language": {"code": "ba", "name": "Bashkir"}, "word": "отоу"}, {"language": {"code": "bn", "name": "Bengali"}, "word": "জেতা"}, {"language": {"code": "bcl", "name": "Central Bikol"}, "word": "gana"}, {"language": {"code": "bg", "name": "Bulgarian"}, "word": "печеля"}, {"language": {"code": "eo", "name": "Esperanto"}, "word": "gajni"}, {"language": {"code": "fi", "name": "Finnish"}, "word": "voittaa"}, {"language": {"code": "gl", "name": "Galician"}, "word": "gañar